// ============================================================================
// LiquidityFlowAuse — Signal Engine Module
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Module:   Signal Engine
// Version:  LiquidityFlowAuse v1.0.0
//
// Purpose:  Evaluates confluence across the four sibling modules and emits
//           LONG / SHORT signals. Scores each direction SEPARATELY, gates each
//           signal with its OWN score, applies a shared cooldown, and renders
//           the label / alert / alertcondition output.
//
// THE CENTRAL INVARIANT: one score per direction, each built only from
// same-side inputs, each gating only its own signal. An earlier draft computed
// a SINGLE `confidenceScore` shared by both directions, from inputs that do
// not know which direction is being scored: a LONG could earn liquidity points
// for a D1 zone ABOVE price, structure points for a bearish break, and
// imbalance points for a bearish gap — praising the setup for reasons that
// contradict the signal it was gating. Two scores, never one.
//
// Pine:     Pine Script v5. The //@version=5 directive is declared once in the
//           main indicator file (src/liquidityflowause.pine); this module MUST
//           NOT contain an indicator() or study() declaration.
//
// Depends:  Everything read below comes from the four sibling modules, all
//           spliced BEFORE this one by scripts/build.mjs. Nothing is
//           redeclared here — a second declaration of any of these names would
//           be a duplicate-declaration compile error, not a warning.
//
// Usage:    Designed for file concatenation into the main indicator file.
//           For a standalone syntax check in the Pine Editor, wrap the
//           contents of this file in a temporary indicator() declaration.
// ============================================================================

// ─── User Inputs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Declared HERE rather than in src/lib/inputs.pine. That file is listed in the
// build's SOURCES array but does not exist on disk, so the build warns and
// skips it and an input declared only in the spec would never reach the
// script. If that file is later written, these three lines must be DELETED from
// here rather than left in both places.
//
// minConfidence maxval is 110, matching the true maximum score, not 100. Only
// the highest nearby liquidity tier counts (see f_liquidityWeight), so the
// ceiling is 30 + 25 + 30 + 15 + 10 = 110 and the score is not a percentage.

minConfidence      = input.int(70,  "Minimum Confidence Score", minval=0, maxval=110, group="Signal Engine")
signalCooldownBars = input.int(10,  "Signal Cooldown (bars)",  minval=1,               group="Signal Engine")
showSignals        = input.bool(true, "Show Signal Labels",                          group="General")

// ─── Liquidity Tier Weight ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The weight for the highest tier that is nearby: 30 for D1, 20 for 4H, 10 for
// 1H, otherwise 0. A price sitting between a D1 zone and a 4H zone scores 30,
// not 50 — only the most significant tier counts.
//
// A chain of nested ternaries. `else if` would also be legal here; the ternary
// is used so "first match wins" is explicit rather than inferred from block
// indentation.
//
// The function is direction-BLIND by design: it knows a tier, not a side. The
// side is carried by the per-side flags passed in (nearD1LiquidityLong and
// friends), and choosing which side is the caller's job — see the score block.

f_liquidityWeight(bool d1, bool h4, bool h1) =>
    int pts = d1 ? 30 : h4 ? 20 : h1 ? 10 : 0
    pts

// ─── Session Factor ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `sessionScore` DOES NOT EXIST. An earlier draft of the spec referenced one and
// it was never implemented, which is an undefined identifier and a compile
// error. Session Markers exports `sessionStrength` (int) and `sessionMultiplier`
// (float); this module reads the former.
//
// The gate is a binary test on the strength, not the multiplier: a signal is
// suppressed outside a tradable session, whereas the multiplier would have
// scaled it down and let a weak out-of-session setup through at a reduced score.
// Crypto runs 24/7, so the multiplier is deliberately NOT used here.
//
// >= 2 means at least one major session (London or NY) is active. Asia alone
// scores 1 and does not clear this gate.

bool sessionOK = sessionStrength >= 2

// ─── Confidence Scoring ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// TWO SCORES, ONE PER DIRECTION, each built only from same-side inputs and
// compared only against its own signal. Every term below is therefore either
// explicitly per-side or genuinely direction-neutral; nothing that knows a
// direction is allowed into the other side's score.
//
// A score is an int, not a percentage: the maximum is 110, which no percentage
// can express.

int longScore  = 0
int shortScore = 0

// Liquidity — per side, from the per-side tier flags. liquidity-zones.pine
// exports six of them (nearD1/H4/H1 Liquidity Long/Short) precisely so there
// are no shared tier flags to read here; a shared flag is what let a LONG earn
// points for a D1 zone above price.
longScore  += f_liquidityWeight(nearD1LiquidityLong,  nearH4LiquidityLong,  nearH1LiquidityLong)
shortScore += f_liquidityWeight(nearD1LiquidityShort, nearH4LiquidityShort, nearH1LiquidityShort)

// Session — direction-neutral, so both scores see the same points. A session
// says nothing about which way price goes, only about whether it is worth
// trading, so it cannot be attributed to one side. A weaker session scores less
// for both, which raises the bar for both rather than favouring either.
int sessionPoints = inOverlap ? 25 : inLondon or inNY ? 15 : inAsia ? 5 : 0
longScore  += sessionPoints
shortScore += sessionPoints

// Structure — the reversal weight REPLACES the continuation weight; it does not
// add to it. An earlier draft awarded +20 for any break and a further +30 for a
// ChoCh, and since ChoCh is a SUBSET of BoS, every reversal scored 50 points
// for one event. Parentheses are explicit around the ternary: `x += c ? a : b`
// relies on precedence that is not worth relying on in the one place a
// misread would corrupt the score.
if breakUp
    longScore += (structureFlipped ? 30 : 20)
if breakDown
    shortScore += (structureFlipped ? 30 : 20)

// Imbalance — same side only. `or`, not `+`: approach and fill are mutually
// exclusive by construction in imbalance-detector.pine (near* requires an
// untouched gap, in* requires a touched one), so at most one arm is ever true
// and a sum could only ever count a single gap twice.
if nearImbalanceLong or inImbalanceLong
    longScore += 15
if nearImbalanceShort or inImbalanceShort
    shortScore += 15

// Volume — confirms activity without implying a direction, so it scores both
// sides equally. This is the one factor that is neutral for the same structural
// reason the session points are, not by convenience.
if volumeConfirmed
    longScore  += 10
    shortScore += 10

// ─── Cooldown ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Declared BEFORE the signals. The signals below do not carry the
// `and not inCooldown` term themselves, but the fired flags computed from them
// do, and a term referencing inCooldown above its declaration is an undefined
// identifier — a compile error.

var int lastSignalBar = na

// na until the first signal fires. The na guard is required, not defensive:
// bar_index - na is na, and na < signalCooldownBars is na, so without the guard
// inCooldown would be na on every bar before the first signal.
bool inCooldown = not na(lastSignalBar) and (bar_index - lastSignalBar) < signalCooldownBars

// ─── Signals ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each signal is gated by its OWN score. There is no shared confidence number,
// so a setup can never be admitted by the strength of the opposite direction.
//
// `breakUp` is the structure term on its own, not `breakUp or bullishChoCh`:
// Structure Break exposes one break flag plus structureFlipped precisely so a
// single structural event cannot satisfy two terms of the same factor. A
// consumer that wants "structure turned bullish" reads `breakUp and
// structureFlipped` — which is a stricter test, not a second factor.

bool longSignalRaw  = nearLiquidityLong  and sessionOK and
                      (breakUp or nearImbalanceLong or inImbalanceLong) and
                      longScore  >= minConfidence

bool shortSignalRaw = nearLiquidityShort and sessionOK and
                      (breakDown or nearImbalanceShort or inImbalanceShort) and
                      shortScore >= minConfidence

// ─── Directional Exclusivity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// LONG and SHORT are NOT mutually exclusive by construction, and this is the
// fifth module's most dangerous gap. Price sandwiched between an H4 zone below
// and an H4 zone above, with untouched gaps on both sides, in the London/NY
// overlap, on elevated volume, scores 20 + 25 + 15 + 10 = 70 on BOTH sides
// simultaneously. Without this block the indicator would print a LONG label, a
// SHORT label, two alert() calls and two alertconditions on the same bar.
//
// Structure is the tiebreaker, not the score. A sandwiched setup is not
// genuinely two opportunities — it is one ambiguous one, and the only thing
// that resolves it is which way structure already leans. `marketStructure` is
// computed by Structure Break precisely for this, and nothing consumed it before
// now.
//
//   both qualify + structure bullish  → LONG only
//   both qualify + structure bearish  → SHORT only
//   both qualify + structure unestablished (0) → NEITHER. A tie with no trend
//                                    to break it is not a trade.
//   one qualifies                     → that one, structure irrelevant
bool longSignal  = longSignalRaw  and not (shortSignalRaw and marketStructure != 1)
bool shortSignal = shortSignalRaw and not (longSignalRaw  and marketStructure != -1)

// A genuine tie with no structure carries no directional information at all,
// so it is dropped rather than resolved arbitrarily. A tie WITH structure is
// already resolved above.
bool ambiguousTie = longSignalRaw and shortSignalRaw and marketStructure == 0

// The cooldown term lives here rather than in the expressions above, and these
// are what the label, the alert() call and the alertcondition() all consume.
// One snapshot of the decision, taken BEFORE lastSignalBar is updated below, so
// a signal that fires on this bar is still emitted on this bar — gating the
// emitters inline would evaluate the same expression four times and drift the
// moment the order changed.
bool longSignalFired  = longSignal  and not inCooldown
bool shortSignalFired = shortSignal and not inCooldown

// ─── Cooldown Application ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Applied AFTER the signals are evaluated and BEFORE the visual output, so the
// label and the alert on this bar correspond to a lastSignalBar that has
// already been stamped. The two fired flags are snapshots taken above, so
// writing lastSignalBar here cannot retroactively cancel them.
//
// One stamp for both directions: if a bar somehow satisfied both, the cooldown
// starts once, not twice.

if longSignalFired or shortSignalFired
    lastSignalBar := bar_index

// ─── Visual Output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// yloc.belowbar / yloc.abovebar OVERRIDE the y price argument — Pine places
// the label a fixed distance beyond the bar and ignores y. The y argument is
// still required positionally, so `low` / `high` is passed as a placeholder.
// The spec's `low - atrChart * 0.5` has no effect under yloc, and its stated
// rationale — an offset expressed in ATRs so the visual distance is scale
// independent — describes behaviour yloc.belowbar does not have.
//
// showSignals gates the DRAWING only. The alert() calls below are not gated by
// it, so switching labels off silences the chart without blinding an alert
// configuration that already exists.
//
// One label per signal, and a signal is cooldown-gated, so this is inherently
// rare and is not a consumer of the max_labels_count=500 budget.
//
// bar_index, not barIndex: the latter is a TradingView alert-message
// placeholder variable that does not exist in script scope.

if longSignalFired and showSignals
    label.new(bar_index, low,
         "LONG\nScore: " + str.tostring(longScore) + "/110",
         style=label.style_label_up,
         color=color.new(color.green, 20),
         textcolor=color.white,
         size=size.normal,
         yloc=yloc.belowbar)

if shortSignalFired and showSignals
    label.new(bar_index, high,
         "SHORT\nScore: " + str.tostring(shortScore) + "/110",
         style=label.style_label_down,
         color=color.new(color.red, 20),
         textcolor=color.white,
         size=size.normal,
         yloc=yloc.abovebar)

// ─── Alert Output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// alert() carries the live score because its message is a SERIES string, built
// on this bar from longScore / shortScore. The alertcondition() calls below
// cannot: their message must be a const string, so only TradingView's own
// {{...}} placeholders interpolate and none of them exposes the score. See the
// note on that block.
//
// These are NOT gated by showSignals — see the note on the visual output.

if longSignalFired
    alert("LiquidityFlowAuse LONG signal on " + syminfo.ticker + " | Score: " + str.tostring(longScore) + "/110", alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

if shortSignalFired
    alert("LiquidityFlowAuse SHORT signal on " + syminfo.ticker + " | Score: " + str.tostring(shortScore) + "/110", alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

// ─── Alert Conditions (spec 10.2) ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ALERT NAMES ARE UNCHANGED. They are the user-facing identifiers anyone who
// has already configured an alert in TradingView is keyed on; only the
// conditions behind them are defined here.
//
// THE TWO SIGNAL ALERTS ARE GATED ON THE FIRED FLAGS, not on raw longSignal /
// shortSignal. The spec's 10.2 reads the raw signals, which fires an
// alertcondition on every bar the confluence holds — including every bar inside
// the cooldown that 7.5 exists to suppress, defeating it entirely. Gating on
// the fired flags is the only reading consistent with 7.5.
//
// THE MESSAGES CARRY NO SCORE. 10.2 writes "Score: {{close}}%", but an
// alertcondition message must be a const string and {{close}} is a PRICE, not
// a score, so that placeholder would print a number the alert has nothing to do
// with. The score-carrying alert for these two events is the alert() call
// above, which can interpolate it.

// The four structure conditions add structureFlipped rather than relying on a
// separate bullishChoCh / bearishChoCh flag, so BoS and ChoCh are genuinely
// distinct conditions instead of four alerts that all fire on the same break.

alertcondition(longSignalFired,  "LF_LongSignal",  "LiquidityFlowAuse LONG signal on {{ticker}}")
alertcondition(shortSignalFired, "LF_ShortSignal", "LiquidityFlowAuse SHORT signal on {{ticker}}")

alertcondition(breakUp   and not structureFlipped, "LF_BullishBoS",   "Bullish BoS on {{ticker}}")
alertcondition(breakDown and not structureFlipped, "LF_BearishBoS",   "Bearish BoS on {{ticker}}")
alertcondition(breakUp   and structureFlipped,     "LF_BullishChoCh", "Bullish ChoCh on {{ticker}}")
alertcondition(breakDown and structureFlipped,     "LF_BearishChoCh", "Bearish ChoCh on {{ticker}}")

// Liquidity approach and sweep. Both fire on the TRANSITION INTO the state,
// read through the [1] historical reference, so they report the edge rather
// than every bar the state holds — a zone can be near for ten bars and that is
// one event, not ten.
//
// On bar 0 the [1] value is na, `not na` is false, and both conditions
// evaluate false, so there is no bar-0 artifact. The swept* flags are read here
// and nowhere else in the module: liquidity-zones.pine keeps a swept zone
// ALIVE precisely so the sweep can be reported, and scoring swept* as a
// confluence term would double-count the event against near* — they are
// mutually exclusive by construction in that module.

bool liquidityTestLong   = nearLiquidityLong  and not nearLiquidityLong[1]
bool liquidityTestShort  = nearLiquidityShort and not nearLiquidityShort[1]
bool liquiditySweepLong  = sweptLong  and not sweptLong[1]
bool liquiditySweepShort = sweptShort and not sweptShort[1]

alertcondition(liquidityTestLong,   "LF_LiquidityTest",  "Price approaching LONG liquidity on {{ticker}}")
alertcondition(liquidityTestShort,  "LF_LiquidityTest",  "Price approaching SHORT liquidity on {{ticker}}")
alertcondition(liquiditySweepLong,  "LF_LiquiditySweep", "LONG liquidity swept on {{ticker}}")
alertcondition(liquiditySweepShort, "LF_LiquiditySweep", "SHORT liquidity swept on {{ticker}}")

// Overlap start: the opening edge only, behind the same `and inOverlap` guard
// the session boundary lines use. ta.change() fires on BOTH edges, so without
// the conjunct this alerts twice per window — once when the overlap begins and
// once when it ends, which is the bug that guard exists to prevent in the
// boundary lines.

alertcondition(ta.change(inOverlap) and inOverlap, "LF_OverlapStart", "London+NY overlap started on {{ticker}}")

// ─── Module Outputs (spec 7.7) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   longSignalFired   (bool) a LONG passed confluence AND was outside cooldown
//   shortSignalFired  (bool) a SHORT passed confluence AND was outside cooldown
//   longScore         (int)  this direction's score, 0-110
//   shortScore        (int)  this direction's score, 0-110
//
// longScore / shortScore are recomputed each bar, so no var. lastSignalBar is
// a var and persists.
//
// NOTHING CONSUMES THESE. This is the root module: there is no module after it
// in the SOURCES array, so no downstream diagnostic will report a wrong
// identifier, a reversed side or a miscounted weight. Every export is the last
// thing checked, so it is the thing that has to be checked by reading.
